# Regression (ritz-task-1267): the lexer's cooked-string buffer must not
# have a fixed capacity.
#
# `struct Lexer` used to carry a fixed `string_buf: [8192]i8` arena shared by
# EVERY string literal in a file, and each write was guarded by
# `if lex.string_pos < MAX_STRING_LEN`.  Once a file's literals totalled more
# than 8 KiB of cooked bytes the guard silently stopped writing: the literal
# straddling the boundary came out truncated, and every literal after it came
# out with `string_len == 0` — an empty `@.str.N` global.
#
# No diagnostic, no error, just wrong output.  ritz1 compiling its own
# `emitter.ritz` crossed 8 KiB inside `register_builtin_structs` and so
# emitted a struct whose name was three NUL bytes, plus a `.ll` missing its
# `; ModuleID` header — which is what blocked bootstrap stage 4 once the
# PHI-predecessor bug was out of the way.
#
# Fix: the arena is gone.  The scratch buffer grows on demand and each
# finished literal is copied to its own exact-sized heap allocation, so the
# only limit on how much string data a file may contain is memory.
#
# This file carries 160 literals of 80 bytes each = 12800 cooked bytes,
# comfortably past the old 8192-byte cliff.  Under the old lexer everything
# past literal ~102 lexed as the empty string and the assertions below fail.

import ritzlib.sys
import ritzlib.io
import ritzlib.str


# Sum the lengths of a lot of long literals.  Every one of these must survive
# lexing intact; the old fixed arena dropped the tail.
fn total_literal_bytes() -> i64
    var total: i64 = 0
    total = total + strlen(c"0000-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx")
    total = total + strlen(c"0001-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx")
